    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [2, 4, 1, 3, 5]  ->  3   (2>1, 4>1, 4>3)\nnums = [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]  ->  10\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4]     ->  0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: count while merging"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Split the array in half. Every inversion is either **inside the left half**, **inside the right half**, or **across** (left value bigger than a right value). The first two are counted recursively. For the third, sort both halves — merge sort does that anyway — and look at the merge: when the merge takes `a[j]` from the right half because it's smaller than `a[i]`, it's also smaller than **every value still waiting in the left half** (they're sorted, all at least `a[i]`). That's `mid − i` inversions counted in one step, instead of one at a time."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: check every pair"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function countInversions(nums) {\n  let count = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n      if (nums[i] > nums[j]) count++;\n    }\n  }\n  return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def count_inversions(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    return sum(1 for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n) if nums[i] > nums[j])"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "long countInversions(int[] nums) {\n    long count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n            if (nums[i] > nums[j]) count++;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "long long countInversions(vector<int>& nums) {\n    long long count = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n            if (nums[i] > nums[j]) count++;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n²), space O(1).**"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Merge sort that counts"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "count-inversions",
      "caption": "Each time the merge takes a value from the right half, add how many values are still waiting on the left."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function countInversions(nums) {\n  const a = [...nums];\n  const sortCount = (lo, hi) => {                 // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the inversions inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n    let count = sortCount(lo, mid) + sortCount(mid, hi);\n    const merged = [];\n    let i = lo, j = mid;\n    while (i < mid && j < hi) {\n      if (a[i] <= a[j]) merged.push(a[i++]);\n      else {\n        count += mid - i;                         // a[j] is smaller than everything left in the left half\n        merged.push(a[j++]);\n      }\n    }\n    while (i < mid) merged.push(a[i++]);\n    while (j < hi) merged.push(a[j++]);\n    for (let k = 0; k < merged.length; k++) a[lo + k] = merged[k];\n    return count;\n  };\n  return sortCount(0, a.length);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def count_inversions(nums):\n    a = list(nums)\n\n    def sort_count(lo, hi):               # sorts a[lo:hi], returns the inversions inside it\n        if hi - lo <= 1:\n            return 0\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        count = sort_count(lo, mid) + sort_count(mid, hi)\n        merged, i, j = [], lo, mid\n        while i < mid and j < hi:\n            if a[i] <= a[j]:\n                merged.append(a[i]); i += 1\n            else:\n                count += mid - i          # a[j] is smaller than everything left in the left half\n                merged.append(a[j]); j += 1\n        merged += a[i:mid] + a[j:hi]\n        a[lo:hi] = merged\n        return count\n\n    return sort_count(0, len(a))"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] a;\n\nlong countInversions(int[] nums) {\n    a = nums.clone();\n    return sortCount(0, a.length);\n}\n\nlong sortCount(int lo, int hi) {                  // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the inversions inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;\n    long count = sortCount(lo, mid) + sortCount(mid, hi);\n    int[] merged = new int[hi - lo];\n    int i = lo, j = mid, k = 0;\n    while (i < mid && j < hi) {\n        if (a[i] <= a[j]) merged[k++] = a[i++];\n        else {\n            count += mid - i;                     // a[j] is smaller than everything left in the left half\n            merged[k++] = a[j++];\n        }\n    }\n    while (i < mid) merged[k++] = a[i++];\n    while (j < hi) merged[k++] = a[j++];\n    System.arraycopy(merged, 0, a, lo, merged.length);\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "long long sortCount(vector<int>& a, int lo, int hi) {   // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the inversions inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n    long long count = sortCount(a, lo, mid) + sortCount(a, mid, hi);\n    vector<int> merged;\n    int i = lo, j = mid;\n    while (i < mid && j < hi) {\n        if (a[i] <= a[j]) merged.push_back(a[i++]);\n        else {\n            count += mid - i;                     // a[j] is smaller than everything left in the left half\n            merged.push_back(a[j++]);\n        }\n    }\n    while (i < mid) merged.push_back(a[i++]);\n    while (j < hi) merged.push_back(a[j++]);\n    copy(merged.begin(), merged.end(), a.begin() + lo);\n    return count;\n}\n\nlong long countInversions(vector<int> nums) {\n    return sortCount(nums, 0, nums.size());\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log n), space O(n).** It's merge sort with one extra addition, so it costs the same. Use a 64-bit counter: n = 10⁵ can have about 5 × 10⁹ inversions. And `<=` matters — equal values are **not** an inversion, so a tie takes from the left without counting."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: check every pair",
          "time": "O(n²)",
          "timeWhy": "Every pair `i < j` is checked: about **n²/2**.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just a counter."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Merge sort that counts",
          "time": "O(n log n)",
          "timeWhy": "**log n** levels of merging, each O(n), with one extra addition per merge step.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **merge buffer**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Inversions also measure work: insertion sort and bubble sort make exactly one swap or shift per inversion."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Sorted** — 0. **Reversed** — n(n − 1)/2.",
        "**Equal values** — `[2, 2]` has 0 inversions.",
        "**Large n** — the count overflows a 32-bit int.",
        "**Copying the input** — count on a copy if the caller's array must not be sorted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Reverse Pairs** — count pairs with nums[i] > 2 · nums[j]: a separate counting pass before each merge. Next lesson.",
        "**Count of Smaller Numbers After Self** — the same merge, keeping a count per original index.",
        "**Global and Local Inversions** — are all inversions between neighbours? A one-pass check, no sorting needed."
      ]
    }
